[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known. Read report by report, it
holds two problems. Each now has its own ticket:

- [luca-gugs/what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/luca-gugs/what-it-shows/issues/1): **day and month
  swapped.** A day-first date was read month first ("Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July").
- [luca-gugs/what-it-shows#3](https://github.com/luca-gugs/what-it-shows/issues/3): **the date moves
  one day.** No swap at all ("Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday"; "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
  Client in Toronto got Monday."). It looks like a time-zone conversion of the booking date.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. The queued fix covers the swap. The one-day moves stay open until #3 is fixed.
</Callout>

## What the desk's table shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

How to read it against the tickets:

- **`parser` and `hint` are one problem, #1.** The table splits them by channel and booking form.
  The reports in both rows show the same swap, whichever way they came in; the booking-v3 form's
  `MM/DD/YYYY` hint is part of the fix, not a problem of its own.
- **The footnote is #3.** The table counts the one-day moves under `parser` because of their tenant.
  Swapping day and month never moves a date by one day, so the swap fix cannot close them.
- **#1 covers the reports in both rows that give both dates, swapped. #3 covers the footnote.**
- The "tagged as" column is intake's guess from the reporter's browser. It does not tell the two
  problems apart, and neither does the subject line. Read the body.

## How the count was made

1. Take the export of #4471 and keep one report per id. The ticket system exports pages that
   overlap, so some reports come twice.
2. Take out the reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`): #3.
3. Of the rest, #1 counts the reports that give both dates and show day and month swapped. A few
   only say the day was wrong; they are counted in neither ticket.

## What this changes

- #4471 is not "known, one fix queued". The queued fix covers #1. #3 needs its own fix and its own
  owner.
- Merging on the subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) cannot keep
  these apart. That decision stays as it is; a new record should replace it.

